  "messages": [
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Choose one next planning step using the local decision below. Return exactly one listed statement and stop. No other planning choices have been supplied.\n{\"context\": \"Contributors have recorded return or donation preferences; a closing collection period precedes the arranged donation pickup, and labeled goods can be stored overnight.\", \"decision\": \"How should unclaimed goods be processed after the fair closes?\", \"goal\": \"Help usable household goods reach interested neighbors while keeping contributions, claims, and final collection understandable.\", \"problem\": \"Neighbors are organizing a one-day fair for clean, usable household goods that people no longer need. Giving and taking are both optional, and no payment or matching contribution is required. Volunteers have intake space, category tables, reserve shelves, a holding area, carts, and simple item records. Contributors choose in advance whether unclaimed goods should be returned during the closing collection period or donated through an arranged pickup.\"}\nCANDIDATE STATEMENTS:\nVolunteers process one display zone at a time, handing recorded return goods to waiting contributors and packing donations beside them. The room clears progressively while the existing preference records determine each item’s destination throughout the closing collection period.\nContributors collect their recorded return items during the closing period, after which volunteers group the remaining donation goods. Direct collection removes many items before final sorting, leaving volunteers a smaller set to prepare for the arranged pickup.\nVolunteers sort all unclaimed goods by recorded return or donation preference before contributors enter the closing collection period. Returned goods are easy to find, and the donation pile is already separated for the later arranged pickup."
    }
  ],
